<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-ultrametrics" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Ultrametrics
  </title>
  
  <idx>
    ultrametric
  </idx>
  
  <idx>
    ultrametric space
  </idx>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A real-valued function <m> \rho \colon X^{ 2 } \to \R </m> on the Cartesian square <m> X^{ 2 } </m> of a set <m> X </m> is called an <term> ultrametric </term> on <m> X </m>, which is called the <term> ground set </term> and whose elements are called <term> points </term> if it satisfies the following conditions:
      
      <ul>
        <li>
          <title>
            Non-degeneracy
          </title>
          
          <p>
            For any points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>, <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 0 </m> if and only if <m> x_{ 1 } = x_{ 2 } </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>
            Symmetry
          </title>
          
          <p>
            <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 1 } } </m> for any points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>
            Strong triangle inequality
          </title>
          
          <p>
            <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } \leq \max \of{ \set{ \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } , \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } } } </m> for any points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } \in X </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      
      If a real-valued function <m> \rho \colon X^{ 2 } \to \R </m> is an ultrametric on <m> X </m>, then the ordered pair <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m> is called an ultrametric space.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that any ultrametric is a metric.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        An ultrametric is non-degenerate and symmetric, and so it remains only to show that it satisfies the triangle inequality. We first observe that given points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m> in an ultrametric space <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m>,
        
        <md>
          \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = \max \of{ \set{ \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } , \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 1 } } } } \geq \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 1 } } = 0
        </md>;
        
        that is, ultrametrics take only positive values. This implies that
        
        <md>
          \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } \leq \max \of{ \set{ \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } , \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } } } \leq \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } }
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } \in X </m>, so that <m> \rho </m> satisfies the triangle inequality and is therefore a metric on <m> X </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Fix a prime number <m> p \in \N </m>. The <term> <m> p </m>-adic valuation </term> <m> \abs{ \oparg }_{ p } \colon \Q \to \Q </m> is a rational-valued function defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          \abs{ q }_{ p } =
            \begin{cases}
              0 \amp q = 0 \\
              p^{ -k_{ p } \of{ q } } \amp q \neq 0
            \end{cases}
        </md>,
        
        where <m> k_{ p } \of{ q } \in \Z </m> is the unique integer so that <m> q = p^{ k_{ p } \of{ q } } \of{ \frac{ a }{ b } } </m> for some integers <m> a , b \in \Z </m> which are not divisible by <m> p </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Prove that the <m> p </m>-adic valuation <m> \abs{ \oparg }_{ p } </m> induces an ultrametric <m> \rho_{ p } \colon \Q^{ 2 } \to \R </m> defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          \rho_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } } = \abs{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } }_{ p }
        </md>.
        
        This ultrametric is called the <term> <m> p </m>-adic metric </term> on <m> \Q </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        First let <m> q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } \in \Q </m> be rational numbers, and suppose that <m> q_{ 1 } = q_{ 2 } </m>. Then
        
        <md>
          \rho_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } } = \abs{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } }_{ p } = \abs{ 0 }_{ p } = 0
        </md>.
        
        Conversely, now suppose that <m> q_{ 1 } \neq q_{ 2 } </m>. Then <m> q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } \neq 0 </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \rho_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } } = \abs{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } }_{ p } = p^{ -k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } } } \neq 0
        </md>.
        
        Since <m> \rho_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } } = 0 </m> if and only if <m> q_{ 1 } = q_{ 2 } </m>, <m> \rho_{ p } </m> is non-degenerate.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Now let <m> q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } \in \Q </m>, and observe that if
        
        <md>
          q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } = p^{ k } \of{ \frac{ a }{ b } }
        </md>
        
        for some integers <m> a , b \in \Z </m> which are not divisible by <m> p </m>, then
        
        <md>
          q_{ 2 } - q_{ 1 } = p^{ k } \of{ \frac{ -a }{ b } }
        </md>,
        
        and <m> p </m> does not divide <m> -a </m> or <m> b </m>. Thus <m> k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } } = k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 2 } - q_{ 1 } } </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \rho_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } } \amp = \abs{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } }_{ p } = p^{ -k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = p^{ -k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 2 } - q_{ 1 } } } = \abs{ q_{ 2 } - q_{ 1 } }_{ p } = \rho_{ p } \of{ q_{ 2 } , q_{ 1 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        We conclude that <m> \rho_{ p } </m> is symmetric.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Finally, let <m> q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } , q_{ 3 } \in \Q </m>, and write
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } \amp = p^{ j } \of{ \frac{ a }{ b } } \amp q_{ 2 } - q_{ 3 } \amp = p^{ k } \of{ \frac{ c }{ d } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for some integers <m> j , k \in \Z </m> and some integers <m> a , b , c , d \in \Z </m> which are not divisible by <m> p </m>. We observe that if <m> j \leq k </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            q_{ 1 } - q_{ 3 } \amp = q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } + q_{ 2 } - q_{ 3 } = p^{ j } \of{ \frac{ a }{ b } } + p^{ k } \of{ \frac{ c }{ d } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = p^{ j } \of{ \frac{ a }{ b } + p^{ k - j } \of{ \frac{ c }{ d } } } = p^{ j } \of{ \frac{ a d + p^{ k - j } b c }{ b d } }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        where <m> p </m> does not divide <m> a d + p^{ k - j } b c </m> or <m> b d </m>. On the other hand, if <m> k \leq j </m>, then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            q_{ 1 } - q_{ 3 } \amp = q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } + q_{ 2 } - q_{ 3 } = p^{ j } \of{ \frac{ a }{ b } } + p^{ k } \of{ \frac{ c }{ d } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = p^{ k } \of{ p^{ j - k } \of{ \frac{ a }{ b } } + \frac{ c }{ d } } = p^{ j } \of{ \frac{ p^{ j - k } a d + b c }{ b d } }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        where <m> p </m> does not divide <m> p^{ j - k } a d + b c </m> or <m> b d </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that
        
        <md>
          k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 3 } } = \min \of{ \set{ k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } } , k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 2 } - q_{ 3 } } } }
        </md>,
        
        so that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \rho_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } , q_{ 3 } } \amp = \abs{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 3 } }_{ p } = p^{ -k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 3 } } } = p^{ -\min \of{ \set{ k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } } , k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 2 } - q_{ 3 } } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = p^{ \max \of{ \set{ -k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } } , -k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 2 } - q_{ 3 } } } } } = \max \of{ \set{ p^{ -k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } } } , p^{ -k_{ p } \of{ q_{ 2 } - q_{ 3 } } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \max \of{ \set{ \abs{ q_{ 1 } - q_{ 2 } }_{ p } , \abs{ q_{ 2 } - q_{ 3 } }_{ p } } } = \max \of{ \set{ \rho_{ p } \of{ q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } } , \rho_{ p } \of{ q_{ 2 } , q_{ 3 } } } }
          </mrow>
        </md>;
        
        that is, <m> \rho_{ p } </m> satisfies the strong triangle inequality.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Since the <m> p </m>-adic metric <m> \rho_{ p } \colon \Q \times \Q \to \R </m> is non-degenerate, symmetric, and satisfies the strong triangle inequality, it is an ultrametric on <m> \Q </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } \in X </m> in an ultrametric space <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m>, at least two of the distances <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } </m>, <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m>, and <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } </m> are equal.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        This follows from applications of the strong triangle inequality. In particular, suppose that <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \neq \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m>. Up to renaming <m> x_{ 1 } </m>, <m> x_{ 2 } </m>, and <m> x_{ 3 } </m>, we may suppose that <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m>. Then
        
        <md>
          \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } \leq \max \of{ \set{ \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } , \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } } } = \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } }
        </md>.
        
        Both <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m> and <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } \leq \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \max \of{ \set{ \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } , \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } } } \leq \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } }
        </md>.
        
        On the other hand,
        
        <md>
          \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } \leq \max \of{ \set{ \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 1 } } , \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } } } = \max \of{ \set{ \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } , \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } } }
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } = \max \of{ \set{ \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } , \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } } } </m>. But we assumed that <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \neq \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m>, and so <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } = \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <conclusion>
    <p>
      The geometry of non-Archimedean metric spaces differs in several ways from our intuitions about Euclidean geometry informed by our experience of distance in the physical world. We will see several of these differences over the course of this chapter.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</exercise>
